Date: Thu, 27 May 93 11:10:27 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: ECON/SPACE: Sell the Universe Whole

Thanks to James A. Donald for pointing out the error in the JLE paper
I referenced.  To bad an Australian didn't review the paper before
publication.  However, I still think the claim that homesteading is
"the way private property rights are always established" is much too
strong.  And I'm a bit surprised to hear the argument "but that's the
way its always been done" from Extropians.  Auctions are not coersive
and arguably better for everyone than squatting.  Maybe they aren't
feasible or desireable, but let's hear more specific reasons on these
points.

Regarding Dani Eder's and Nick Szabo's comments, I did not mean to
claim that any one nation held space claims.  Rather, I'd be surprised
if the nations who signed the treaty would sit idly by while some
supposedly "private" group started to do something like what they had
forbidden each other to do.  Nick's example of Clarke orbits is
instructive; nations did take over this resource, instead of allowing
private homesteading.

Robin Hanson

