It's not that we need another category, the category defines itself.
The theory helps develop what I (semi-jokingly) call The Unified
Brain Theory, in that the Gestalt Theory, helps explain a common
mechanism that describes the root "cause" of autism, schizophrenia,
and what is commonly called creativity (eg inventiveness, innovation,
inspiration) and indeed does better explain what has previously been
an area of study that was more voodoo than anything.

The Gestalt Theory attempts to help those people that already know they
don't fit in with the majority, the so-called INTJ people, yes, even
people who call themselves Extropians.

>2) How do you test for G?

Right now, it's through self identification; I am working on a variant
of the MMPI that will hopefully test for G qualities.  I also theorize
that the Gestalt Mechanism will show up on MRI.  People who are Gestalt,
can ask of themselves (and already do) "Do I feel different than most?"
and "Have I always felt that way?" and answer yes.

>3) How do you exploit G?

Better yet, perhaps, how could G exploit themselves?  G is able to do
all those things traditionally associated with inventors etc.  Companies
can use G in any role that demands an individual who can see the big
picture; society the same way.  Gestalts are organizers, innovators;
G can accomplish anything they set their minds to.

>4) How does a G minimize the GLC? (or should they want to?)

I would think we all would want to reduce conflict in our lives; it
gets in the way of what we are really trying to accomplish.  G can
reduce the main source of conflict in their lives, by understanding
it, and then developing techniques to deal with the conflict.

Another question not asked, is what does all this have to do with
Extropianism?  I am saying that if there could be a common mindset
that could be ascribed to extropes, then perhaps extropes
could be of literally "like minds" in that they share the Gestalt
Mechanism; they are Gestalts, not so much as by a shared belief
system, but by having the same approach to analyzing diverse pieces
of information, and coming to the same conclusion(s).

