Some points from the previous message, continued...

The best analogy I have come up with, for the Gestalt Theory,
is that of handedness.

Left handedness, is a genetic "condition" with the left handed
"mechanism" housed in the brain.  Exactly how this mechanism
works, or where in the DNA, the section(s) that determine left
handedness is located, are (currently) unknown.

If we examine the end result of this condition, we find that it
has some impact on the individuals that have this mechanism in
common.

In the past (and in the present) left handed people have been
labelled such extremes as "stupid" all the way to "evil".  In
some societies (the US included) left handed people (LHP) have
had to hide their LH (Left Handedness) and use their right hands.

In a right society, the LHP is "abnormal"; they are surrounded
with tools that don't work well for them; day to day thnigs that
they do become ever more conflictive for them; they could start
laying blame on themselves, when they feel clumsy, through no true
fault of their own.

As you can imagine, this could lead to an internalization process
occurring, behavior changes, psychological conditions that are
specific to LHP.  And so on...

So it is with HSG (Homo Sapien Gestalt) and their lives.

But Gestalt, by being self aware of their "condition" can affect
changes in their own lives.  Gestalts have always been with "us";
throughout history there are examples of Gestalts that have "done
good" (and "done bad" as well); it is possible that Gestalts have
been responsible for some the most important changes that society
has taken.  Thomas Edison, Benjamin Franklin, Thomas Jefferson;
the list goes on.

A whole generation of self aware, self actualized Gestalts could
accomplish anything they wanted.

Regards, Ron | rcarter@nyx.cs.du.edu

