Date: Thu, 27 May 93 17:49:55 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: ECON/SPACE: Sell the Universe Whole

Perry writes:
>How do I auction off something that no one owns?

I would have thought this was clear from the discussion, but it occurs
to me than most folks may be skimming.  So let me summarize.

------- Sell The Universe Whole Proposal ---------

I propose that the United Nations, or most nations that signed
previous space treaties, sign a new treaty agreeing to auction the
universe from the moon on out as a single unit (excepting rights to
near earth sunlight).  The proceeds would then be immediately
distributed among the signatory nations in proportion to population,
or any other agreeable distribution rule.

The universe owner would eventually break up the universe into smaller
chunks and auction off these chunks, and these new universe-part
owners could similarly auction off smaller chunks.  To discourage
monopolies, a rule might be added that no commercial use could be made
of any chunk until it had been sold at an auction where no owner held
more than 10% (or X%) of the market value of the chunks sold at that
auction open to all comers.

No explicit enforcement mechanism is envisioned, as with most
treaties.  Violators would, as usual, create excuses for military
actions against them.

-------------------------------------------------

Bidders would have incentives to not buy universe chunks they could
not plausibly defend.  But near the margin, folks should defer to
established property rights, for fear of retaliation and censure.  

In the worse case, it all reverts to self-enforced homesteading
anyway.  Since the universe-owner's claim is relatively worthless in
this case, this owner will have incentives to avoid this situation.

But in better cases, the value to us of the universe existing out
there is not entirely spent in a race to get there first, and we can
all be better off.

Robin Hanson 


