Date: Thu, 27 May 93 23:28:56 MDT
From: Mark_Muhlestein@Novell.COM (Mark Muhlestein)
Subject: Is AI's failure evidence of souls?

David:

I have found this thread very stimulating, and I hope you will indulge
me in a little further discussion.

I wrote:

>It seems to me that David has a valid point, but that the result is
>very weak.  The reason for this is that in a Bayesian analysis, as I
>understand it, it is normally considered correct to assign equal
>probabilities to all unknown components, the sum adding up to one.

To which you replied:

>No. That does not work because the procedure you describe is not well
>defined. Suppose, for example, we are considering a variable X that has an
>unknown value between 0 and 1. The equivalent of the procedure you describe
>would be to assume a uniform probability density. But that gives a
>non-uniform density for X squared--about whose distribution we also know
>nothing.

My argument does not depend on any particular probability density.

Let's define "C" as the probability that AI cannot be achieved.  Let's
also agree that "C" increases whenever a prediction of AI fails.

Now, associated with "C" is a set "F" of mutually exclusive explanations
of why AI cannot be achieved.  From your comments, you seem to be
equating "F" with another set "S" which consists of all non-mechanistic
explanations of consciousness.

My contention is that "F" is not identical to "S" and can be decomposed
into many mutually exclusive subsets, such as:

  S: your non-mechanistic "soul" explanation
  G: a god forbids it
  A: alien interference
  L: incredibly bad luck
  P: negative psychic energy
  etc.

Since there is no prima facie evidence for any of these explanations,
it would seem unduly cavalier to automatically assign zero probability
to those explanations you don't like, since it is purely a subjective
judgement call to assign probabilities to each of these explanations in
the first place, and different people would assign different
probabilities.  Also, how could you be sure you had covered all the
important possibilities?  ("Of course you can't simulate intelligence!
The processor that is running our simulated universe is programmed to
detect such attempts and doom them to failure!") Yet since all
probabilities must add up to "C" each of the other explanations dilutes
the probability of "S" to some degree.

You then state:

>Bayesian analysis, as I understand it, starts with some set of prior
>probabilities based on whatever information you already have and describes
>how to modify them on the basis of further information. I do not think one
>can make sense out of a zero knowledge starting point--that is one of the
>problem with Rawls.

I agree completely with this.  In doing some reading on Bayesian
analysis, I have discovered that it was Pierre Laplace who first
postulated the controversial "principle of indifference," which states
that in total ignorance we should allocate probabilities equally among
alternatives.[1]  With something as vague as my argument above, it does
seem irrational to assign equal probability to each member of an
indefinite list of hypothetical explanations.

Laplace's idea seems useful in some well defined circumstances, but as you
say, with zero knowledge to begin with it is problematic to say how
useful such a Bayesian analysis really is.

In any case, even conceding this point, I still believe the gist of my
argument stands.

As to my own standpoint, I find myself more agreeing with Perry that the
evidence is currently very strong that a mechanistic explanation of
consciousness will emerge.  But I also agree with Derek that "... the
mechanistic view of mind is just a theory, and with a lack of hard
evidence either for or against, it is accepted or rejected largely on
faith."

To me, the truth of the matter will be astounding, whatever it
ultimately turns out to be.

Mark_Muhlestein@novell.com

[1] _Fuzzy Logic_, McNeill & Freiberger 1993, p 178.

