Date: Thu, 27 May 93 23:34:37 PDT
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: PRESS: Space Colonies etc unlikely according to AP

This probability-based calculation is an interesting variant on the
discussion we had here some time back about the possibility that our
distant descendants may be simulating the earlier universe, and we may
be living in that simulation.

Hans Moravec wrote about this in the previous issue of Extropy, arguing
that there would actually be much more (virtual) time spent in the innumerable
simulations than in the single reality.  Hence the odds are overwhelming
that we are actually living in such a simulation.

Gott in Princeton is using a similar argument, that the chance of an entity
being alive at a location in spacetime is proportional to the density of
living entities there.  But I can think of a couple of counter-arguments
even if we accept this methodology (which most people won't, of course).

First, it's possible that many of the people in the future are actually
ourselves, living forever and making many copies.  Our lives now are the
foundations of their memories, and the weight of all those people remembering
these distant days increases the probability that we will have experienced
them.

Another possibility is that Moravec is right and our descendants run a lot
of simulations.  What will they want to simulate?  Well, we've all seen
the graph of technology level against time.  It looks like:

                                ----------------------------------
                                |
                                |
                                |
                                |
---------------------------------

At the left is prehistory, and at the right is mature technology.  We
are on the vertical line, which spans only a few thousand or perhaps
tens of thousand years.  Naturally, this would be where most of the
simulations will run since that is the exciting time.  So people during
this era get more weight than people at other eras, even though there
are fewer of them.

Fuzzily,

Hal

