From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: ECON: Property as Schelling point

I can see that there are cases where Schelling points can apply even when
communication is possible.  The classic "divide-the-dollar" game, where two
people get a dollar if they can mutually agree on how much of it each one
gets, is an example.  The 50-50 split has a certain obviousness to it that
will tend to drive the agreements to that point.

In this sense, divisions of new property need to have a similar obviousness
so that people will accept them and will believe that other people will
enforce them.

I'm not sure that human nature is such that giving the whole universe to one
organization falls into that category, though.  In fact, from this perspective
I'd say that even if there are some economic advantages to this approach
the proposal does not have credibility in terms of human nature.  I don't
think people will accept this division of the property nor will they believe
that the organization can enforce it.  The very asymmetry of this solution
argues against it.

If you want a Schelling point I'd say the equal global giveaway of random
shares is the closest to the symmetrical solution in the divide-the-dollar
game.

Hal

